const e=[{id:"l-homicide",moduleId:"m04-criminal-law",title:"Homicide: the structure of sections 299 and 300",minutes:30,summary:"Culpable homicide is the big category, murder is a smaller category sitting inside it, and the exceptions push a case back down again.",plants:[],prepares:["ps-homicide-01"],reading:[{bookId:"yeo-morgan-chan-criminal-law",where:"the chapters on homicide and on the general exceptions"}],source:"Penal Code (Act 574) ss 299, 300 and Exceptions 1 and 4, 302, 304; PP v Visuvanathan [1978] 1 MLJ 159; Virsa Singh v State of Punjab AIR 1958 SC 465 (Supreme Court of India).",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Read ss 299, 300, 302 and 304 in a current reprint of Act 574. The sentencing provisions were amended in 2023 and must not be quoted from memory. Confirm the Visuvanathan citation and look for a more recent Federal Court statement of the s 300(c) test.",plate:"lamp-blow",plateCaption:"One blow with something picked up in the moment. Limb (c) is the one that fits these facts, and the one students skip past.",readMinutes:11,sections:[{id:"sec-l-homicide-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Start from the section rather than a theory of killing; the Code defines its own offences.",h:"Start with the Code, not with a theory",body:[{t:"p",text:"A man dies after a fight outside a shop, and somebody is arrested that same night. What exactly should he be charged with, and who decides?"},{t:"p",text:"In Malaysia the answer sits in a book called the Penal Code. Every offence has its own section, and each section writes in the state of mind it needs — no more and no less."},{t:"p",text:"So the first move in any criminal problem is always the same. Find the section, read it, and do not reason from a general feeling about guilt."},{t:"p",text:"Unlawful killing shows this better than anything else in the Code. The structure is clean once you have seen it, and thoroughly confusing if you arrive with English categories in your head."}]},{id:"sec-l-homicide-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Three steps in order: culpable homicide under s 299, then murder under s 300, then any Exception.",anchor:"fig-l-homicide-0",h:"Three steps, always in this order",body:[{t:"p",text:"Section 299 says what **culpable homicide** is, which is the Code's name for unlawful killing. Section 300 then says when culpable homicide counts as **murder**, and the exceptions to section 300 say when it drops back down again."},{t:"rule",text:"Section 299: culpable homicide is causing death by an act done with the intention of causing death, or with the intention of causing such bodily injury as is likely to cause death, or with the knowledge that the act is likely to cause death.",source:"Penal Code, s 299"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: it is culpable homicide if the person meant to kill, or meant to cause an injury that was likely to kill. It is also culpable homicide if they knew that what they were doing was likely to kill."},{t:"p",text:"Culpable homicide is the big category and murder is a smaller category sitting inside it. Every murder is culpable homicide, but not every culpable homicide is murder."},{t:"p",text:"So never ask “is this murder or culpable homicide?” as though the two were rivals. Ask first whether it is culpable homicide at all, and then ask whether it is the kind that section 300 turns into murder."},{t:"diagram",kind:"flow",title:"The route through a homicide problem",caption:"Always in this order. Going straight to section 300 because someone died inverts the structure and usually picks the wrong limb.",alt:"Three steps: establish culpable homicide under s 299, then ask whether a limb of s 300 elevates it to murder, then whether an Exception brings it back down.",steps:[{label:"Culpable homicide?",note:"s 299"},{label:"Murder?",note:"one of the four limbs of s 300"},{label:"Any Exception?",note:"back down to culpable homicide"}]},{t:"p",text:"Every limb of the analysis assumes something that is easy to skip past. The prosecution must establish that the accused's own act caused the death, and where an intervening cause is genuinely arguable that question has to be answered separately."}]},{id:"sec-l-homicide-2",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Four limbs make it murder, and (c) — an injury sufficient in the ordinary course of nature — does most of the work.",h:"The four ways it becomes murder",body:[{t:"example",text:"Rosli and Daud argue outside a kedai runcit late one evening. Rosli picks up a metal pipe lying by the drain and swings it once at Daud's head, and Daud dies that night. Rosli tells the police that he never meant to kill anybody."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Rosli swung the pipe once, and he tells the police he never meant to kill anybody. Before you read on — does that keep this out of murder?",options:["Yes — murder means meaning to kill, and he did not","No — it can be murder if he meant to cause the wound he caused","It turns on whether Rosli knew that a pipe could kill a man"],answer:1,reveal:"The second one. Hold on to the first answer, though. It is what the word murder means in ordinary speech, and the Code is about to take that meaning away from you. Section 300 has four routes in, and only one of them asks about an intention to kill. The third answer is the near miss. What Rosli knew matters under some limbs, and not under the limb that will catch him."},{t:"p",text:"Section 300 has four limbs, lettered (a) to (d), and any one of them turns culpable homicide into murder."},{t:"list",items:["(a) The act is done with the intention of causing death — that is, meaning to kill.","(b) The act is done meaning to cause an injury that the accused knows is likely to kill this particular person.","(c) The act is done meaning to cause the injury actually caused, and that injury is enough, in the ordinary course of nature, to cause death.","(d) The act is so obviously dangerous that it must in all probability cause death, or cause an injury likely to cause death. The accused had no excuse for running that risk."]},{t:"rule",text:"Section 300(c) is two questions: did the accused intend to inflict the injury actually inflicted, and is that injury objectively sufficient in the ordinary course of nature to cause death.",source:"Penal Code, s 300(c); PP v Visuvanathan [1978] 1 MLJ 159, applying Virsa Singh v State of Punjab AIR 1958 SC 465 (Supreme Court of India)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: did you mean to cause the wound you caused, and would a wound like that normally kill?"},{t:"p",text:"The second question has nothing to do with what was in the accused's head, because it is answered by the doctor who examined the body. That is why limb (c) is the one the prosecution reaches for most often."},{t:"p",text:"Go back to Rosli. He meant to strike Daud's head with the pipe, and that is the injury Daud suffered, and a pathologist will say that a blow like that normally kills. Limb (c) is made out, and the fact that Rosli did not realise it could kill is simply not the question."},{t:"caution",text:"It is tempting to run limb (a) because a person died. On a single blow with something picked up in the moment, (a) is hard to prove and (c) is straightforward. Choosing the wrong limb means proving a harder case than the law asks for."}]},{id:"sec-l-homicide-3",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Exceptions push murder back down: provocation, exceeding private defence, sudden fight.",h:"The exceptions: murder pushed back down",body:[{t:"p",text:"Suppose a limb of section 300 is made out on the facts. The offence is then murder, unless one of the exceptions to the section applies."},{t:"p",text:"The exceptions do not set anybody free, and that is the thing to hold on to. They reduce murder to culpable homicide not amounting to murder, which is a lesser offence carrying a lesser sentence. Treating provocation as a way out altogether is the commonest mistake in this area, because it is a reduction and not an escape."},{t:"rule",text:"Exception 1: culpable homicide is not murder if the offender, whilst deprived of the power of self-control by grave and sudden provocation, causes the death of the person who gave the provocation.",source:"Penal Code, s 300, Exception 1"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: you were pushed past self-control by something serious that had just happened, and you killed the person who did it."},{t:"p",text:"Two words carry all the weight and they fail in different ways. **Grave** asks how serious the provocation was, while **sudden** asks whether there was time for temper to cool."},{t:"example",text:"Halim's neighbour has insulted his late mother, on and off, for two years. One evening Halim takes a parang and kills him for it. The provocation may well be grave, but it is hard to call it sudden, because two years is a long time in which to cool down."},{t:"p",text:"An Explanation to the section makes whether provocation was grave and sudden a question of fact, and that is what allows words alone to count as provocation."},{t:"caution",text:"Three provisos cut the exception down, and any one of them will sink it. The provocation must not have been sought out or brought on by the offender himself. It must not come from something done in obedience to the law, or by a public servant acting lawfully. Nor may it come from somebody who is lawfully defending themselves."},{t:"p",text:"Exception 4 covers a sudden fight, where the killing happens in the heat of passion on a sudden quarrel. The offender must not take undue advantage or act in a cruel or unusual way, and the exception is worth checking on any facts involving a scuffle."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Grave and sudden provocation is made out on the facts. What happens to the accused?",options:["He walks free — provocation is a complete defence","The offence drops to culpable homicide not amounting to murder","He is still convicted of murder, and only the sentence changes"],answer:1,why:"Exception 1 reduces the offence. It does not excuse the killing, and it does not merely soften the sentence. The first answer is the commonest mistake in this area, because the word *defence* sounds like a way out. The third is the other one. The conviction itself changes, from section 302 to section 304."}]},{id:"sec-l-homicide-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Sections 302 and 304 carry the punishments, and the sentencing law changed in 2023.",h:"Sentence",body:[{t:"p",text:"Section 302 punishes murder, and section 304 punishes culpable homicide not amounting to murder."},{t:"p",text:"Section 304 splits by state of mind, because the intention limb and the knowledge limb carry different maximum sentences. So the limb you settled on earlier is what decides which maximum applies."},{t:"caution",text:"The mandatory death penalty for murder was abolished in 2023, and sentencing is now at the court's discretion. Do not state a range from memory or from an older book — read the current provision. This is the single most likely thing on this page to have moved since it was written."}]},{id:"sec-l-homicide-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"One blow, a clear post-mortem, and arguable provocation — the problem is built to test exactly this.",h:"What to do with this",body:[{t:"p",text:"Work the problem question, which is built to test exactly this. It gives you a single blow, a clear pathologist's report, and provocation that is arguably grave and doubtfully sudden."},{t:"p",text:"The work is in taking section 300(c) as two separate questions, and then arguing both sides of suddenness rather than asserting one of them."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qhm1",q:"How do sections 299 and 300 fit together?",options:["They are two separate offences of equal weight","Section 300 sets the sentence for section 299","Section 299 applies only where section 300 does not","Culpable homicide is the big category; murder is a smaller one inside it"],answer:3,why:"Every murder is culpable homicide. Not every culpable homicide is murder.",source:"Penal Code ss 299 and 300"},{id:"qhm2",q:"Section 300(c) asks two questions. What are they?",options:["Did the accused intend death, and did death follow","Was the act very dangerous, and did the accused know it","Was there provocation, and was it grave and sudden","Did the accused intend the injury actually caused, and is that injury enough in the ordinary course of nature to cause death"],answer:3,why:"The second question is answered by the pathologist, not by the accused. That is why (c) is often easier to prove than (a).",source:"Penal Code s 300(c)"},{id:"qhm3",q:"One blow is struck with an object picked up in the moment, and the victim dies. Which limb is the natural one to run?",options:["(a)","(b)","(d)","(c)"],answer:3,why:"It is tempting to run (a) because someone died. On these facts an intention to kill is hard to prove, and (c) is straightforward.",source:"Penal Code s 300(c)"},{id:"qhm4",q:"What does Exception 1 to section 300 require?",options:["Grave and sudden provocation that destroyed self-control, and the death of the person who gave it","Any provocation at all","Provocation together with planning","Provocation given by someone else"],answer:0,why:"And it does not set the accused free. It reduces murder to culpable homicide not amounting to murder.",source:"Penal Code, Exception 1 to s 300"},{id:"qhm5",q:"What should you never state from memory about the sentence for murder?",options:["Which court tries it","How the offence is classified","The range — the mandatory death penalty went in 2023 and sentencing is now discretionary","The elements of the offence"],answer:2,why:"Sentencing provisions move. Always read a current source.",source:"Penal Code s 302; the sentencing provisions were amended in 2023 and must be read in a current reprint"}]},{id:"l-property-offences",moduleId:"m04-criminal-law",title:"Property offences: how possession was obtained",minutes:25,summary:"Theft, criminal breach of trust and cheating look alike from the outside. One question separates them, and it is not what was taken.",plants:[],prepares:["ps-property-01"],reading:[{bookId:"yeo-morgan-chan-criminal-law",where:"the chapters on offences against property"}],source:"Penal Code (Act 574) ss 378, 379, 405, 408, 415, 420.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Read ss 378, 405, 408, 415 and 420 in a current reprint of Act 574. The classification reasoning is settled; the section numbers for the aggravated and punishment provisions are what to confirm.",plate:"shop",plateCaption:"One counter, three routes out, three offences. The question is never what was taken but how possession was obtained.",readMinutes:10,sections:[{id:"sec-l-property-offences-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Three incidents in one shop on one afternoon, and three different offences.",h:"One Saturday in a hardware shop",body:[{t:"p",text:"Siti works the till at a small hardware shop in Klang, and three things happen there in one afternoon."},{t:"list",items:["A customer pays RM200 in cash, and Siti puts it straight into her own pocket and records no sale.","On her way out she takes a drill worth RM150 from the display shelf.","She tells the owner that a supplier needs RM300 up front, but there is no such supplier and she keeps the money."]},{t:"predict",prompt:"Three dishonest acts, one Saturday afternoon. Before you read on — which of them is theft?",options:["All three — every time, she took something that was not hers","The drill only","The drill, and the RM200 out of the till"],answer:1,reveal:"Only the drill. If you said all three, that is how the afternoon looks from outside, and it is the charge a hurried officer would bring. The Code does not sort offences by what was taken. It sorts them by how the property got into her hands, and Siti got hold of those three things in three different ways."},{t:"p",text:"All three are dishonest, and in all three the property belonged to somebody else. Yet these are three different offences under three different sections, and an officer who charges all of them as theft has got two of them wrong."}]},{id:"sec-l-property-offences-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"The question is never what was taken but how the accused came to have it.",anchor:"fig-l-property-offences-0",h:"The question that separates them",body:[{t:"p",text:"The tempting question is “what did she take?”, and that is the wrong question. The right one is how she came to have the property in the first place."},{t:"list",items:["Taken out of somebody else's possession, and without the consent of that person — that is theft.","Received lawfully, and then treated by her as though it were her own — that is criminal breach of trust.","Handed over willingly by its owner, but only because she lied to him — that is cheating."]},{t:"p",text:"Who owns the thing is not the test, because in all three of them the property belongs to somebody else. That common feature is what makes each of them an offence, and possession and consent are what tell the three apart."},{t:"diagram",kind:"branch",title:"One question separates the three offences",caption:"Not what was taken, and not how much it was worth. How possession was obtained.",alt:"A question about how possession was obtained, branching three ways. Never given: theft under section 378, moved out of possession without consent. Given lawfully: criminal breach of trust under section 405, entrusted and then misappropriated. Given but by a trick: cheating under section 415, where delivery was induced by deception.",question:"How did the accused come to have the property?",branches:[{cond:"never given",label:"Theft",note:"s 378 — moved out of possession without consent"},{cond:"given lawfully",label:"Criminal breach of trust",note:"s 405 — entrusted, then misappropriated"},{cond:"given, but by a trick",label:"Cheating",note:"s 415 — delivery induced by deception"}]},{t:"p",text:"One further element runs through all three offences and is easy to overlook. Every one of them requires **dishonesty**, so an honest belief that you were entitled to the property is capable of answering any of the three charges."}]},{id:"sec-l-property-offences-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Theft under s 378: moved out of someone's possession, without consent, dishonestly.",h:"Theft",body:[{t:"rule",text:"Section 378: whoever, intending to take dishonestly any movable property out of the possession of any person without that person's consent, moves that property in order to such taking, commits theft.",source:"Penal Code, s 378"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: you take a thing that is not yours out of somebody else's hands, without their agreement, and you move it."},{t:"p",text:"Two elements do the work here: the property must come out of somebody else's possession, and it must be taken without the consent of the person who had it."},{t:"p",text:"Two smaller points matter as well, and both of them catch people out. Only movable property can be stolen, so land cannot be, and the offence is complete the moment the property is moved with a view to taking it. Getting away with the goods afterwards is not required at all."},{t:"example",text:"The drill was sitting on the shop's shelf and in the shop's possession, and nobody had handed it to Siti or put her in charge of it. Taking it off the shelf and walking out with it is therefore theft."}]},{id:"sec-l-property-offences-3",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Criminal breach of trust under s 405: it came to them lawfully, and was then misappropriated.",h:"Criminal breach of trust",body:[{t:"rule",text:"Section 405: whoever, being in any manner entrusted with property, or with any dominion over property, dishonestly misappropriates or converts it to his own use, or dishonestly uses or disposes of it in violation of any direction of law or legal contract, commits criminal breach of trust.",source:"Penal Code, s 405"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: the property was put into your hands for a purpose, and you kept it or used it as though it were your own."},{t:"p",text:"**Entrusted** means that the property was put into your hands to hold or to handle on somebody else's behalf."},{t:"p",text:"Here the accused starts out with the property lawfully, so nothing comes out of anybody else's possession, because she already had it herself. The wrong lies in keeping for herself, or using as her own, the thing she was trusted with."},{t:"example",text:"Siti was allowed to take payment from customers, so the RM200 reached her hands lawfully, in her job as cashier. Pocketing it is criminal breach of trust rather than theft, because nothing was taken out of anybody else's possession."},{t:"p",text:"Where the accused is a clerk or a servant, a heavier form of the offence applies, and that is the form that fits most employee cases."},{t:"caution",text:"Whether an employee has possession of what passes through their hands, or only custody of it, is a genuine question that turns on what exactly they were entrusted with. Being a cashier does not put the whole shop in Siti's hands, so argue the point rather than simply assuming it."}]},{id:"sec-l-property-offences-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Cheating under s 415: consent was given, but a deception induced it.",h:"Cheating",body:[{t:"rule",text:"Section 415: whoever, by deceiving any person, fraudulently or dishonestly induces the person so deceived to deliver any property to any person, commits cheating.",source:"Penal Code, s 415"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: you tell somebody a lie, and because of the lie they hand something over."},{t:"p",text:"Cheating is the odd one out, because here the victim agrees and the property is handed over willingly. What poisons the agreement is the lie that produced it, and the lie is the thing you have to prove."},{t:"example",text:"The owner handed Siti RM300 for a supplier who did not exist. He agreed to part with the money, but he agreed to it only because of the lie he had been told, and that is cheating."},{t:"p",text:"Where the lie leads to property being handed over, the punishment section is section 420 rather than the general one. Charging under the wrong punishment section is a real error and not a matter of style."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Puan Lim hands her neighbour RM500 and asks him to pay her electricity bill. He spends it on himself instead. Which offence fits?",options:["Theft — he used money that was not his","Criminal breach of trust — the money was put into his hands for a purpose","Cheating — he must have lied to get hold of it"],answer:1,why:"The money reached him lawfully, and for a stated purpose, so nothing came out of anybody's possession. That is section 405. Theft is the trap, because spending someone else's money feels exactly like stealing it. Cheating fails too: nothing in the facts says a lie is what made her hand the money over."}]},{id:"sec-l-property-offences-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Take each incident separately and ask the one question again; a bundle of facts is not one charge.",h:"Facts that arrive in a bundle",body:[{t:"p",text:"Real cases come as one story with several incidents inside it, and it is tempting to label them all the same way. Take each incident on its own and ask the one question of each."},{t:"caution",text:"One set of facts can support more than one charge, and charging in the alternative is ordinary practice. Saying which charge is the better one, and why, is worth more than picking one and moving on."}]},{id:"sec-l-property-offences-6",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The problem gives you an officer who wants to charge all three as theft, and he is wrong twice.",h:"What to do with this",body:[{t:"p",text:"The problem question gives you a cashier, three incidents on a single Saturday, and an investigating officer who wants to charge all three of them as theft. Only one of the three actually is."},{t:"p",text:"The work is in the reason, and the reason is one line long. How did the accused come to have the property in the first place?"}]}],quiz:[{id:"qpo1",q:"What single question separates theft, criminal breach of trust and cheating?",options:["How the accused came to have the property","What was taken","How much it was worth","Whether the accused gained anything"],answer:0,why:"Taken without consent. Received lawfully, then kept. Or handed over because of a lie.",source:"Penal Code ss 378, 405 and 415"},{id:"qpo2",q:"Property is received lawfully and then treated as the receiver's own. Which offence?",options:["Theft","Criminal breach of trust","Cheating","Extortion"],answer:1,why:"Section 405. Entrusted with the property, then dishonestly taking it for yourself.",source:"Penal Code s 405"},{id:"qpo3",q:"Which section defines theft?",options:["Section 378","Section 405","Section 415","Section 300"],answer:0,why:"Dishonestly moving movable property out of another's possession, without their consent.",source:"Penal Code s 378"},{id:"qpo4",q:"Someone hands over property, but only because they were lied to. Which offence?",options:["Theft","Cheating","Criminal breach of trust","Robbery"],answer:1,why:"Section 415. The victim consents, but the lie is what produced the consent.",source:"Penal Code s 415"},{id:"qpo5",q:"Can one set of facts support more than one charge?",options:["No, the charges rule each other out","Only with the court's leave","Only in the Sessions Court","Yes, and charging in the alternative is ordinary practice"],answer:3,why:"Saying which charge is the better one, and why, is part of the answer.",source:"Standard charging practice; charges may be laid in the alternative"}]},{id:"l-non-fatal",moduleId:"m04-criminal-law",title:"Offences against the person short of death",summary:"The Penal Code grades harm rather than treating all injury alike. The grading decides the offence, and the offence decides almost everything else.",minutes:28,readMinutes:9,reading:[{statuteId:"penal-code",where:"the chapter dealing with offences affecting the human body — read the definitions first, then the grading provisions, and note the section numbers yourself with the date you checked"}],source:"The structure of graded offences against the person is that of the Penal Code. No section number is stated, because this corpus has verified only Penal Code ss 6, 99(3) and (4), 299, 300, 378, 405 and 415, none of which are the provisions dealt with here.",verify:"Open the Penal Code as currently published by the Attorney General's Chambers, check the currency date on the front matter, and find the provisions yourself. This lesson deliberately names none.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",sections:[{id:"sec-l-non-fatal-0",h:"A ladder of harm",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"The Code sorts injury by seriousness, so identifying the grade is the first analytical step.",body:[{t:"p",text:"The homicide lesson showed a structure: a wide category, a narrower one inside it, exceptions pushing a case back down."},{t:"p",text:"Offences short of death are organised on a similar principle. Harm is graded, and the grade you are on decides which offence is charged."},{t:"p",text:"So the first question is not whether the accused did something wrong. It is how serious the harm was, in the Code's own terms."},{t:"caution",text:"The Code's categories are defined terms, not ordinary English. A word like hurt has a definition in the statute, and reasoning from the everyday meaning will put you on the wrong rung."}]},{id:"sec-l-non-fatal-1",h:"Harm, and the aggravating features",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Offences are aggravated by the means used, the intention behind them, and the circumstances of the act.",body:[{t:"p",text:"Above the base grade the Code adds aggravating features, and they are worth knowing as categories."},{t:"list",items:["The seriousness of the injury itself","The means used — whether a weapon or dangerous instrument was involved","The intention or knowledge with which it was done","The circumstances, including who the victim was and why"]},{t:"p",text:"Each feature typically has its own provision, so an accurate answer names the offence that matches the features present rather than the most serious one available."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Two accused cause identical injuries; one used a weapon. Same offence?",options:["Yes — the harm is what matters","No — the means used is a separate aggravating feature with its own provision"],answer:1,reveal:"Grading by outcome alone would make the Code much shorter and much blunter. The separate provisions exist precisely so that identical harm caused in different ways can be charged differently."}]},{id:"sec-l-non-fatal-2",h:"The mental element still has to be found",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Serious injury does not by itself establish the intention or knowledge the offence requires.",body:[{t:"p",text:"It is tempting to reason backwards from a bad injury to a guilty mind. The Code does not permit it."},{t:"p",text:"Each offence states its own mental element, and it has to be established on the evidence like anything else."},{t:"p",text:"So an answer must identify the mental element for the specific provision it is relying on, and apply it to the facts rather than inferring it from the harm."},{t:"p",text:"The general exceptions in Chapter IV apply here as they do elsewhere, and the burden on them sits with the accused."}]},{id:"sec-l-non-fatal-3",h:"Reading the provisions for yourself",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"This lesson names no section numbers, because this corpus has not verified them.",body:[{t:"p",text:"You will have noticed that no section number appears above."},{t:"p",text:"That is deliberate. This project's rule is that a provision is cited only where someone has checked it against the published Act, and nobody here has checked the provisions on hurt."},{t:"p",text:"So the instruction is to open the Penal Code, find the chapter dealing with offences affecting the human body, and read the definitions and the grading provisions in order."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Why does this lesson give you a structure but no section numbers?",options:["Because the numbers change too often to be useful","Because nobody working on this corpus has verified them against the published Act, and an unchecked citation is worse than none"],answer:1,why:"A wrong section number on a law site looks exactly like a right one and survives every automated check. Naming the chapter and sending you to read it is the honest version, and it also builds the habit the whole reading tier exists for."}]}],quiz:[{id:"q-crim-nf-1",q:"What decides which offence against the person is charged?",options:["The victim's opinion","The length of the trial","The grade of harm, together with aggravating features such as means and intention","Whether the accused has previous convictions"],answer:2,why:"The Code grades harm and then adds separate provisions for aggravating features. Matching the facts to the right combination is the analysis."},{id:"q-crim-nf-2",q:"Why can the mental element not be inferred from the seriousness of the injury?",options:["Because injuries are hard to assess","Because each offence states its own mental element, which must be established on the evidence","Because intention is presumed in all cases","Because the burden lies on the accused"],answer:1,why:"Reasoning backwards from harm to state of mind skips the element the provision actually requires. Serious harm can be caused with a mental state the charged offence does not cover."}]},{id:"l-inchoate-participation",moduleId:"m04-criminal-law",title:"Reaching people who did not do it themselves",summary:"Criminal liability extends to those who tried, those who helped, and those who acted together. Each is a different route and they are not interchangeable.",minutes:28,readMinutes:9,reading:[{statuteId:"penal-code",where:"the provisions on abetment, on common intention, and on attempt — find each, read it, and record the section numbers with the date you checked"}],source:"Attempt, abetment and common intention as structured by the Penal Code. No section number is stated, because this corpus has not verified the relevant provisions.",verify:"Read the abetment, common intention and attempt provisions in the current published Penal Code and check the reprint's currency date. This lesson names no section on purpose.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",sections:[{id:"sec-l-inchoate-participation-0",h:"Three routes, not one",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Attempt, abetment and common intention extend liability in different directions and require different things.",body:[{t:"p",text:"Most offences describe someone doing something. The Code also reaches people who did not."},{t:"p",text:"There are three distinct routes and they are commonly muddled."},{t:"list",items:["Attempt — the offence was not completed","Abetment — someone helped, instigated or conspired","Common intention — several acted together in furtherance of a shared intention"]},{t:"p",text:"They are not degrees of the same thing. An answer that says the accused was involved without naming the route has not identified the offence."}]},{id:"sec-l-inchoate-participation-1",h:"Attempt: past preparation",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"An attempt requires an act beyond mere preparation, together with the intention for the full offence.",body:[{t:"p",text:"Attempt asks how far along the accused had got."},{t:"p",text:"Thinking about an offence is not an attempt. Buying what you need is usually still preparation."},{t:"p",text:"Somewhere after that the conduct becomes an attempt, and the line is the hard part — it is a question of fact argued on the specifics."},{t:"p",text:"The mental element is demanding: an attempt generally requires the intention to commit the full offence, even where the full offence could be committed with something less."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Why might attempt require a higher mental element than the completed offence?",options:["It does not — the elements are always identical","Because without the result, intention is what identifies the conduct as criminal at all"],answer:1,reveal:"When the harm has not happened, the act on its own may be entirely innocent. What makes buying an ordinary object into an attempt is the purpose behind it, so intention carries more weight than it does when a result has occurred."}]},{id:"sec-l-inchoate-participation-2",h:"Abetment and common intention are different",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Abetment is about helping or instigating another's offence; common intention is about acting together on a shared plan.",body:[{t:"p",text:"These two are confused more than any other pair in this area."},{t:"p",text:"Abetment looks at what one person did in relation to another's offence — instigating it, helping it, agreeing to it."},{t:"p",text:"Common intention looks at a group acting in furtherance of an intention they shared, and can make each liable for what was done in furtherance of it."},{t:"p",text:"The practical difference is what you must prove. For abetment, the act of assistance or instigation. For common intention, the shared intention and participation in furtherance of it."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Your accused drove others to a location knowing what they planned. Which route do you analyse?",options:["Common intention only","Both — driving may be an act of assistance, and there may also be a shared intention; they require different proof"],answer:1,why:"The same facts can support either or both, and they are established differently. Choosing one without considering the other risks running the route the evidence supports least."}]},{id:"sec-l-inchoate-participation-3",h:"Where the sections are",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The provisions are in the Penal Code; this lesson names none, for the same reason as the last.",body:[{t:"p",text:"As with the previous lesson, no section numbers appear here."},{t:"p",text:"The Code deals with abetment and with common intention in their own places, and attempt both generally and within particular offences."},{t:"p",text:"Find them, read them, and write the numbers down with the date you checked."}]}],quiz:[{id:"q-crim-inch-1",q:"What distinguishes an attempt from preparation?",options:["An act going beyond preparation towards commission, with the intention to commit the full offence","The time elapsed","Whether a weapon was bought","Whether anyone was harmed"],answer:0,why:"Preparation is not criminal as an attempt. The question is whether conduct has moved beyond it, and that is argued on the specific facts rather than settled by a formula."},{id:"q-crim-inch-2",q:"How does abetment differ from common intention?",options:["They are the same offence","Abetment concerns helping or instigating another's offence; common intention concerns acting together in furtherance of a shared intention","Abetment applies only to property offences","Common intention requires a written agreement"],answer:1,why:"They reach different conduct and require different proof. The same facts may support both, which is why each has to be analysed rather than assumed."}]},{id:"l-sentencing",moduleId:"m04-criminal-law",title:"After conviction: what a court may impose",summary:"Conviction settles liability, not consequence. Sentencing is a separate exercise with its own purposes, its own material and its own arguments.",minutes:26,readMinutes:8,reading:[{statuteId:"criminal-procedure-code",where:"the provisions governing sentence — find what a court may impose and any procedural requirements, and note the sections yourself"},{bookId:"janab-criminal-procedure",where:"the treatment of sentencing practice, for how the factors are actually argued"}],source:"The purposes of sentencing and the standard aggravating and mitigating factors are general criminal law doctrine. Statutory limits are set by the Penal Code and the Criminal Procedure Code; no section, penalty or minimum is stated here, because this corpus has verified none of them.",verify:"Check what the statute permits for the specific offence in the current published Penal Code and Criminal Procedure Code, including any mandatory minimum, before relying on anything in this lesson to advise on outcome.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",sections:[{id:"sec-l-sentencing-0",h:"A separate stage",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Sentencing follows conviction as a distinct exercise, argued on different material.",body:[{t:"p",text:"A criminal case does not end at conviction. It moves to a stage with its own rules."},{t:"p",text:"The material differs. Evidence of the offence is settled; what matters now is the offender, the circumstances, and anything urged in mitigation."},{t:"p",text:"So the advocacy differs too. An argument that the accused did not do it has no place here, and running it wastes the one opportunity to affect the outcome."},{t:"caution",text:"Students routinely treat sentencing as an afterthought. For a convicted client it is the entire remaining question."}]},{id:"sec-l-sentencing-1",h:"Purposes that pull in different directions",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Punishment, deterrence, protection and rehabilitation can point to different sentences on the same facts.",body:[{t:"p",text:"Sentencing serves several purposes at once and they do not agree."},{t:"list",items:["Retribution — a penalty proportionate to what was done","Deterrence — of this offender, and of others","Protection of the public","Rehabilitation of the offender"]},{t:"p",text:"On the same facts, deterrence may argue for a heavier sentence than retribution would allow, and rehabilitation for a lighter one than protection would permit."},{t:"predict",prompt:"A first offender commits an offence that is becoming common in the area. Which purposes conflict?",options:["None — they all point the same way","Deterrence argues for severity to address prevalence; rehabilitation and proportionality argue against punishing this individual for a trend"],answer:1,reveal:"This is the sharpest recurring conflict in sentencing, and it is where general deterrence is most criticised: it uses one offender to send a message to others. Naming the conflict is a better answer than pretending the purposes align."}]},{id:"sec-l-sentencing-2",h:"Aggravation and mitigation",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Factors are grouped around the offence and the offender, and the second is where mitigation usually lies.",body:[{t:"p",text:"Factors sort into two families and it helps to keep them apart."},{t:"p",text:"Those concerning the offence: planning, the harm caused, the victim's vulnerability, the use of a weapon, the offender's role."},{t:"p",text:"Those concerning the offender: previous record or its absence, age, a plea and when it was entered, cooperation, personal circumstances."},{t:"p",text:"A plea in mitigation that only asserts the offence was not serious is arguing against the conviction. The stronger material is usually in the second family."}]},{id:"sec-l-sentencing-3",h:"What limits the court",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A court sentences within limits set by statute, and this lesson states none of them.",body:[{t:"p",text:"A court is not at large. Statute sets what may be imposed for an offence, and sometimes fixes a minimum."},{t:"p",text:"Those limits are in the Penal Code and the Criminal Procedure Code, and this lesson names no section and no penalty."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Before advising a convicted client on likely sentence, what must you establish first?",options:["The purposes of sentencing","What the statute permits for this offence, including any minimum"],answer:1,why:"Purposes shape an argument within a range. The range itself comes from the statute, and advice given without checking it can promise something the court has no power to give."}]}],quiz:[{id:"q-crim-sent-1",q:"Which sentencing purposes most often conflict?",options:["Retribution and protection","Rehabilitation and protection","None conflict","General deterrence and proportionality to this offender's conduct"],answer:3,why:"General deterrence looks outward to other potential offenders, while proportionality looks at what this offender did. Prevalence of an offence is the situation where the tension is sharpest."},{id:"q-crim-sent-2",q:"What is the weakest form of plea in mitigation?",options:["One relying on the offender's personal circumstances","One mentioning an early plea","One that in substance disputes the conviction","One raising the offender's age"],answer:2,why:"Liability is settled by then. Re-arguing it spends the one chance to influence sentence on a question the court has already decided."}]},{id:"l-general-exceptions",moduleId:"m04-criminal-law",title:"The general exceptions, and who has to prove them",minutes:25,summary:"Chapter IV of the Penal Code applies to every offence in it. If you want the benefit of an exception you must prove it yourself, and the standard is the balance of probabilities.",plants:["ms-exc-01","ms-exc-02","ms-exc-03","ms-exc-04","ms-exc-05"],prepares:[],reading:[{bookId:"yeo-morgan-chan-criminal-law",where:"the chapters on the general exceptions and on private defence"}],source:"Penal Code ss 6, 76-106, and Exception 2 to s 300; Evidence Act 1950 s 105.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Check the current text of ss 82 and 83 for the ages, and check the current Federal Court statement of the standard to which an accused must prove a general exception — the phrase used in the judgments matters more than the paraphrase above.",plate:"shield",plateCaption:"The shield of Chapter IV, and the weight of section 105 — the accused carries it, on the balance of probabilities.",readMinutes:9,sections:[{id:"sec-l-general-exceptions-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Chapter IV applies to every offence in the Code, whether or not the section repeats it.",h:"They apply to every offence",body:[{t:"p",text:"A man pushes a snatch thief off a motorbike and the thief is badly hurt. Has he committed an offence? The definition of the offence will not tell you. The answer sits in a different part of the Penal Code."},{t:"p",text:"Sections 76 to 106 are called the General Exceptions. They are not defences to particular offences. They apply to every offence in the Code, and to offences under other statutes so far as the Code's general provisions are made applicable."},{t:"p",text:"You will not find any of them mentioned in the section that defines the offence. Section 6 is the reason why."},{t:"rule",text:"Every definition of an offence in the Penal Code is to be understood subject to the exceptions in the Chapter entitled 'General Exceptions', though those exceptions are not repeated in the definition itself.",source:"Penal Code, s 6"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: every offence in the Code is read with the general exceptions attached, even though the definition never mentions them."},{t:"p",text:"So a homicide answer has looked at only half the picture if it discusses the exceptions to section 300 and never asks about private defence or unsoundness of mind."}]},{id:"sec-l-general-exceptions-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Mistake, accident, necessity, infancy, unsoundness of mind, intoxication, consent, private defence.",h:"The main groups",body:[{t:"list",items:["**Mistake and being bound to act (ss 76 and 79)** — a person bound by law to do the act. Or a person who, by a mistake of fact and in good faith, believes himself bound or justified by law. Mistake of *fact*, not of law.","**Judicial and official acts (ss 77 and 78)** — a judge acting judicially, and a person acting under a court's judgment or order.","**Accident (s 80)** — an accident or misfortune, without criminal intention or knowledge, while doing a lawful act by lawful means with proper care and caution.","**Necessity (s 81)** — an act likely to cause harm, done without criminal intention and in good faith, to prevent other harm to person or property.","**Infancy (ss 82 and 83)** — nothing done by a child under ten is an offence. Between ten and twelve it turns on whether the child had attained sufficient maturity of understanding.","**Unsoundness of mind (s 84)** — by reason of unsoundness of mind, the person was incapable of knowing the nature of the act. Or incapable of knowing that it was wrong or contrary to law.","**Intoxication (ss 85 and 86)** — narrow. It covers intoxication caused without the person's knowledge or against his will, and intoxication producing insanity.","**Consent (ss 87 to 92)** — with limits set by age and by the degree of harm.","**Private defence (ss 96 to 106)** — of the body, and of property."]}]},{id:"sec-l-general-exceptions-2",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Private defence has limits: no more harm than necessary, and not where the authorities could be reached.",h:"Private defence",body:[{t:"p",text:"Private defence appears in more problems than any other exception. It is a matter of degree, so it is genuinely arguable. Two ideas hold it in place."},{t:"example",text:"Ah Meng is walking home when a man grabs his bag and shoves him. Ah Meng pushes back and the man falls. That is likely to be within the right. Now change one thing. The man drops the bag and walks off, and Ah Meng chases him and hits him with a helmet. The danger was over. That is a different case."},{t:"p",text:"Section 99 sets the outer edges of the right. It says when there is no right at all, and how far the right can ever go."},{t:"rule",text:"There is no right of private defence against an act which does not reasonably cause the apprehension of death or grievous hurt, if there is time to have recourse to the protection of the public authorities; and the right in no case extends to inflicting more harm than is necessary.",source:"Penal Code, ss 99(3) and 99(4)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: if there was time to go to the authorities and no reasonable fear of death or grievous hurt, there is no right in the first place. And even where the right exists, it never licenses more harm than the situation needs."},{t:"p",text:"Section 100 sets out the cases in which the right of private defence of the body extends to causing death. They include a reasonable apprehension of death, and of grievous hurt, and certain enumerated offences. Section 103 does the same for property."},{t:"predict",prompt:"A man defends himself against an attack, goes further than the danger needed, and kills. He acted in good faith and had no plan. What does the excess do?",options:["Nothing — he went too far, so it is murder","It brings the conviction down to culpable homicide not amounting to murder","It leaves the murder conviction and only softens the sentence"],answer:1,reveal:"Going too far does not simply cancel the defence. Exception 2 to section 300 brings the charge down, so the excess changes the conviction and not only the sentence. If you chose the first answer, you were right that the defence did not stretch that far. The Code just does something else with the excess."},{t:"caution",text:"The commonest error is to treat the right as lost because the response was excessive. Excess goes to whether the right extended *this far*. An accused who exceeds the right, in good faith and without premeditation, may still bring the charge down. Murder becomes culpable homicide not amounting to murder, under Exception 2 to section 300. Those are different convictions and they carry different sentences."},{t:"diagram",kind:"branch",title:"Section 99 asks whether the right exists at all",caption:"Excess does not destroy a right that existed. It goes to whether the right extended this far. Answering the two questions in the wrong order is the commonest error in this area.",alt:"A question — was there a reasonable apprehension of death or grievous hurt, or no time to go to the authorities? — branching two ways. No: section 99 means no right arises, and the enquiry ends. Yes: the right exists, and the next question is how far it extends, with section 100 setting out when it reaches causing death.",question:"Reasonable fear of death or grievous hurt, or no time to reach the authorities?",branches:[{cond:"no",label:"No right arises",note:"s 99 — the enquiry ends here"},{cond:"yes",label:"The right exists — now ask how far",note:"s 100 lists when it reaches causing death"}]}]},{id:"sec-l-general-exceptions-3",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Section 105 puts the burden on the accused, on the balance of probabilities — not on the prosecution.",h:"Who has to prove it",body:[{t:"p",text:"The prosecution must prove the offence. But if you want the benefit of an exception, the work of proving it falls on you."},{t:"rule",text:"When a person is accused of any offence, the burden of proving the existence of circumstances bringing the case within any of the General Exceptions in the Penal Code is upon him, and the court shall presume the absence of those circumstances.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, s 105"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: the court starts by assuming the exception does not apply, and it is for the accused to show that it does."},{t:"p",text:"That is a legal burden on the accused, and it is discharged on the balance of probabilities — more likely than not. It is not the criminal standard."},{t:"p",text:"The prosecution's own burden is untouched by any of this. It must still prove the offence beyond reasonable doubt, and it carries that burden throughout."},{t:"example",text:"Rosli is charged after a fight and says he was acting in self-defence. He does not have to make the court sure of it. He has to make the court think it more likely than not. If he does, the exception is his."},{t:"caution",text:"Do not write that the accused must merely 'raise' the exception, and that the prosecution must then disprove it beyond reasonable doubt. That is the English position on some defences. It is not what section 105 says."},{t:"compare",title:"Two burdens, at two different standards",left:{h:"The prosecution — the offence",items:["Must prove every element of the offence.","Beyond reasonable doubt.","This burden is untouched by any exception, and it never shifts.","It carries that burden throughout."]},right:{h:"The accused — an exception (s 105)",items:["The court starts by assuming the exception does not apply.","A legal burden, on the accused, to show that it does.","On the balance of probabilities — more likely than not.","**Not** merely raising it and leaving the prosecution to disprove it. That is the English position, and it is not ours."]}}]}],quiz:[{id:"qge1",q:"Who has to prove a general exception, and to what standard?",options:["On the prosecution, beyond reasonable doubt","On the accused, beyond reasonable doubt","On the accused, on the balance of probabilities","It is a purely evidential burden"],answer:2,why:"Section 105 puts a legal burden on the accused and directs the court to presume the circumstances are absent.",source:"Evidence Act 1950, s 105"},{id:"qge2",q:"Section 79 of the Penal Code excuses an act done under a mistake of what?",options:["Law","Identity only","Either fact or law","Fact"],answer:3,why:"A mistake of fact, made in good faith, believing oneself justified by law. A mistake about the law does not excuse.",source:"Penal Code s 79"},{id:"qge3",q:"Under s 82, a child under what age cannot commit an offence at all?",options:["Seven","Ten","Twelve","Fourteen"],answer:1,why:"Nothing done by a child under ten is an offence. Between ten and twelve the question is sufficient maturity of understanding.",source:"Penal Code ss 82 and 83"},{id:"qge4",q:"An accused goes further than the right of private defence allowed, and kills. What is the likely effect?",options:["Murder may be reduced to culpable homicide not amounting to murder","The right is simply lost and murder stands","It is a complete defence anyway","It goes only to sentence"],answer:0,why:"Exception 2 to s 300 covers exceeding the right in good faith and without premeditation.",source:"Penal Code, Exception 2 to s 300"},{id:"qge5",q:"Under s 99(3), when is there no right of private defence?",options:["Where there is time to go to the public authorities and no fear of death or grievous hurt","Whenever the attacker is unarmed","Whenever the police could be called","Whenever the accused struck first"],answer:0,why:"Both halves matter. Time to seek protection is not enough on its own if death or grievous hurt is reasonably feared.",source:"Penal Code s 99(3)"},{id:"qge6",q:"Why does Chapter IV matter to a case about a killing?",options:["They apply only to offences against property","The exceptions apply to every offence in the Code, s 300 included","They replace the exceptions to s 300","They apply only in the Sessions Court"],answer:1,why:"Section 6 makes every definition of an offence subject to the general exceptions.",source:"Penal Code s 6"}]}];export{e as default};
